  "id": "DrugBank_DB13874 - MONDO_17893",
  "verbalization": "Enasidenib is associated with Inherited Acute Myeloid Leukemia (AML) through a pathway involving translocations that are commonly found in this disease. The evidence suggests that Enasidenib is indicated for AML with the specific T(8;21)(q22;q22) translocation, which is a hallmark of certain subtypes of AML. This translocation is also linked to the use of decitabine, ivosidenib, and azacitidine in the treatment of this condition. The shared indication for these drugs in the context of AML with the T(8;21)(q22;q22) translocation implies a potential mechanism of action that targets the same molecular pathway. The recurring association between Enasidenib and AML with this translocation, along with the presence of similar indications for other drugs in the same class, suggests that Enasidenib may be a viable treatment option for this specific subset of AML. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of targeting the translocation-driven molecular mechanisms in AML.",
